# CMake expands this template into the build tree's zlib.pc -- the
# configure_file() call in CMakeLists.txt -- and installs the result into
# <libdir>/pkgconfig.  The autotools build (configure plus Makefile.in) and
# win32/Makefile.gcc use the parallel template zlib.pc.in rather than this file,
# so a change to what either one advertises belongs in both.
#
# This descriptor is implementation-agnostic for DYNAMIC linking: the flags below
# describe the C implementation and the memory-safe Rust implementation
# (crates/libz-rs-sys) equally well there, so ZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON needs no variant
# of this file and changes none of the keys below.  What the Rust packaging is
# REQUIRED to reproduce, and what the ZLIB_BUILD_RUST block in CMakeLists.txt
# implements:
#
#   * the staged artifacts are named libz.so and libz.a -- the same names the C
#     targets publish, and that block is ELF-only -- so -lz resolves to either;
#   * the shared object carries SONAME libz.so.1 and the same
#     libz.so -> libz.so.1 -> libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley chain, so a program already
#     linked against libz keeps loading it under the name recorded in its
#     DT_NEEDED entry.  Cargo does not produce that chain: it writes a bare
#     libz.so, so CMakeLists.txt relinks cargo's static archive into the
#     versioned name -- with the same SONAME and the same zlib.map version
#     script the C shared target receives -- and symlinks the other names beside
#     it, in a staging directory of its own rather than in cargo's output
#     directory.  Those files install into the same libdir as the C libraries,
#     from rules declared last, so where both implementations publish a name the
#     Rust file is the one installed;
#   * the exported symbol surface must match the C library's 111 dynamic
#     globals, being 95 functions plus the 16 symbol-version nodes declared in
#     zlib.map.  The CMake path takes that keep-list from the ZEXTERN
#     declarations in zlib.h, localises everything else with objcopy before the
#     shared link, and hands zlib.map to the linker; `make rust-symbols' is the
#     autotools build's nm diff of the same property.  No in-tree automation
#     runs that diff for this build, so read it as the parity requirement rather
#     than as an achieved result;
#   * the installed zlib.h and generated zconf.h are byte-identical, because the
#     Rust build modifies neither header and that block leaves the header, the
#     zlib.pc and the documentation install rules untouched.
#
# Libs: advertising -lz and Cflags: advertising the include directory are part of
# that contract -- do not reword them, and do not add Requires: entries, -lm,
# -lpthread or any Rust-specific flag to them.
#
# ONE PACKAGING DIFFERENCE, AND IT IS REAL: STATIC LINKING.
#
# The C libz.a needs no companion libraries, which is why there is no
# Libs.private: line below and why adding one would be wrong for the C build.  A
# Rust staticlib is different -- it carries the Rust runtime's own dependencies.
# Measured, not assumed, with
# `RUSTFLAGS="--print native-static-libs" cargo build --release -p libz-rs-sys`
# on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu:
#
#     -lgcc_s -lutil -lrt -lpthread -lm -ldl -lc
#
# That set is TARGET-SPECIFIC and must be re-derived per target; it is not a
# constant to copy.  This file advertises none of it, so static consumption of
# the staged Rust libz.a is an OPEN PACKAGING GAP rather than a solved problem.
# How far it bites depends on the consumer, and the honest measurement is that a
# plain cc link of that archive with -lz alone still succeeds here, because the
# compiler driver already puts libgcc and libc on the link line and this glibc
# folds libpthread, libdl, librt and libutil into libc.  It breaks where that
# does not hold -- ld driven directly, -nodefaultlibs, a toolchain belonging to
# another language, an older glibc that ships those four separately, or a target
# whose set simply differs -- and it breaks with nothing in this file to warn
# that the requirement exists.  zlib.pc.in states it as an outright link
# failure; read that as the worst case of this same gap, not as a different one.
#
# It cannot be closed by editing this template, because one file serves both
# implementations and the C build must keep emitting a .pc with no Libs.private:
# at all.  Closing it means having the Rust packaging step emit its own
# descriptor, with Libs.private: populated from
# `rustc --print native-static-libs` for the target being built.  Dynamic
# linking is unaffected either way: the shared object records its own
# dependencies in DT_NEEDED, so -lz alone remains correct there.
#
# Two things about the substitution, for whoever edits this next.  The
# configure_file() call passes @ONLY, so only the at-delimited names are
# substituted and the ${...} references in libdir, sharedlibdir and includedir
# survive into the generated file, for pkg-config itself to resolve at use time
# against the prefix recorded there.  That is what lets a consumer relocate the
# prefix, so do not expand them by hand and do not drop that keyword.  And the
# substitution pass runs over the whole file, comments included: never write one
# of the substitutable names in here, because it would be rewritten here too,
# and one CMake happens not to define expands to nothing at all rather than
# failing.
#
# A KNOWN DIVERGENCE FROM zlib.pc.in, DELIBERATE AND OUT OF SCOPE TO HARMONISE:
# the Version: field below is filled from the version given to project() in
# CMakeLists.txt, which is 1.3.2.1, whereas the configure-generated zlib.pc
# carries ZLIB_VERSION from zlib.h, which is 1.3.2.1-motley.  Both spellings
# predate the Rust port.  The suffixed one is the library's version identity --
# deflateInit_ and inflateInit_ compare a caller's compile-time copy of it --
# while project() accepts numeric components only, so the two cannot be spelled
# alike without changing one of them.  Nothing links differently for it:
# pkg-config only reports this field and answers version queries from it, and
# the Rust staging above publishes the project()-spelled library name alongside
# the header-spelled one, so which spelling a consumer saw never decides which
# file resolves.

prefix=@CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@
exec_prefix=@CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@
libdir=${exec_prefix}/@CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR@
sharedlibdir=${exec_prefix}/@CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR@
includedir=${exec_prefix}/@CMAKE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR@

Name: zlib
Description: zlib compression library
Version: @zlib_VERSION@
License: Zlib

Requires:
Libs: -L${libdir} -L${sharedlibdir} -lz
Cflags: -I${includedir}
